The county sent you to look at the toll books. The lock-keeper did not expect you before morning.

Kitchen

A low room under the lock house, with a black iron stove against the wall. Water knocks against the lock gate.

The lock-keeper sits by the stove and does not look up.

Exits: up, east.

> examine stove
examine stove

The stove is cold.

"Do not touch the stove," the lock-keeper says.

> up
up

Keeper's Room

A narrow room under the roof. A cot, a chair, and an iron hook by the door.

A windlass hangs on the hook by the door.

Exits: down.

> take windlass
take windlass

Taken.

> down
down

Kitchen

A low room under the lock house, with a black iron stove against the wall. Somewhere a rope creaks.

The lock-keeper sits by the stove and does not look up.

Exits: up, east.

> e
e

Lock Chamber

Stone walls drop into the lock. Black water fills the chamber to the brim.

Exits: west.

> open lower paddles
open lower paddles

You fit the windlass and turn. Water roars out under the lower gate.

> wait
wait

Time passes.

> wait
wait

Time passes.

The chamber is empty. Wet ladder rungs go down into the dark.

> open gear housing
open gear housing

You open the gear housing. The gear housing contains an oilcloth packet.

> take pages
take pages

Taken.

> w
w

Kitchen

A low room under the lock house, with a black iron stove against the wall. Water knocks against the lock gate.

The lock-keeper sits by the stove and does not look up.

Exits: up, east.

> examine stove
examine stove

The stove is cold.

"Do not touch the stove," the lock-keeper says.

> accuse keeper
accuse keeper

You have no reason to accuse that.

You lay the pages on the table. The lock-keeper looks at them for a long time.

Committed for trial

At dawn the county takes the lock-keeper to town. The pages go with him, in your coat.